course: books
lesson: 41
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter V, part 1: Dulce Domum (the village windows and the call of home)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: It is mid-December, nearly dark, and Rat and Mole are tramping home across frosty fields after a long day out with Otter. A track turns into a path, the path into a lane, the lane into a road, and the road leads through a little village. All the people are indoors, so the two friends peep in at the lit-up windows: a cat being stroked, a sleepy child carried off to bed, a tired man knocking out his pipe.
explain: Behind one drawn blind they see the shadow of a bird-cage and a little bird who wakes, yawns and tucks his head back in. It feels so cosy that they forget they are outside — until a sting of icy sleet reminds them how cold their toes are and how far they still have to go.
explain: Out on the dark road again, the Rat walks ahead. Suddenly a smell reaches the Mole like an electric shock: it is HOME — his own old home, the one he ran out of on that spring morning and has hardly thought of since. It is calling him, sad but not angry, wanting him back.
explain: «Ratty! Come back! It's my home!» he cries. But the Rat is far ahead, worried about the coming snow, and calls back, «Come on, Mole, we'll come for it tomorrow!» So poor Mole, his heart torn in two, turns his back on his home and follows his friend.
fact: «Dulce Domum» is Latin for «sweet home» — the name of an old school song about going home for the holidays.
fact: The sheep huddle «against the hurdles» (hurdles = movable fence panels of a sheep-pen), stamping and breathing steam into «the frosty air».
fact: Rat and Mole were coming back «after a long day's outing with Otter, hunting and exploring on the wide uplands» where small streams that feed their River begin.
fact: «The animals did not hold with villages» (did not like or approve of them). The Rat says: «At this season of the year they're all safe indoors by this time, sitting round the fire.»
fact: It is «the rapid nightfall of mid-December», with «a first thin fall of powdery snow». The cottage windows glow «dusky orange-red», and most are «innocent of blinds» (have no blinds).
fact: Inside, the people have the «natural grace which goes with perfect unconsciousness of observation» — they look natural because they don't know anyone is watching.
fact: The two friends, «so far from home themselves, had something of wistfulness in their eyes» (wistful = sad and longing).
fact: Behind one blind hangs a bird-cage, «even to yesterday's dull-edged lump of sugar»; the bird yawns «in a bored sort of way» and «settled his head into his back again».
fact: Then «a small sting of frozen sleet on the skin woke them as from a dream». The Mole's thoughts «ran a good deal on supper»; the Rat walks ahead «his shoulders humped, his eyes fixed on the straight grey road».
fact: People, says the writer, have only the word «smell» for all the «delicate thrills which murmur in the nose of the animal night and day, summoning, warning, inciting, repelling».
fact: The smell is «the fine filament, the telegraphic current» (a thin thread, like a telegraph wire's signal). Then: «Home! That was what they meant».
fact: His home had called him «sorrowfully, reproachfully, but with no bitterness or anger; only with plaintive reminder that it was there, and wanted him». (Plaintive = sad-sounding.)
fact: The Rat calls back: «We'll come for it to-morrow, whatever it is you've found. But I daren't stop now—it's late, and the snow's coming on again … And I want your nose, Mole».
fact: «Even under such a test as this his loyalty to his friend stood firm.» With «a wrench that tore his very heartstrings» he follows the Rat, and the little smells «reproached him for his new friendship and his callous forgetfulness» (callous = unfeeling).
fact: Talk in this lesson: «nesh» (North and Midlands England) = feeling the cold easily; «ginnel» (North England) = narrow alley between houses; «I'm dead» = that's so funny; «toque» (Canada) = woolly winter hat; «bairn» (Scotland, North East) = child.
exercise: What month is it in this chapter? | December | June, at the start of summer | March, in early spring | October, in autumn
exercise: Whom had Rat and Mole spent the day with? | The Otter | The Badger, deep in the Wild Wood | Toad, at the Hall | The field-mice
exercise: What did the sheep do as the two animals passed? | Huddled against the hurdles, stamping and steaming | Ran after them all the way to the village | Slept | Bleated a song
exercise: How did the path change as they walked? | Track, path, lane, then a proper road | It got narrower and narrower until it vanished | It turned into a river | It led into the Wild Wood
exercise: «The animals did not hold with villages.» What does it mean? | They didn't like or approve of villages | They couldn't carry villages | They lived in villages | They built villages
exercise: Why does the Rat think it is safe to walk through the village? | At this time of year everyone is indoors by the fire | The village people are all friends of the Rat and wave to him | It is midnight | The Badger owns it
exercise: What do the cottage windows look like from the dark street? | Squares of dusky orange-red | Black and shut tight, with heavy wooden shutters | Blue | Frosted over
exercise: Which of these did they NOT see through the windows? | A dog doing tricks for a biscuit | A cat being stroked | A sleepy child carried off to bed | A man knocking out his pipe
exercise: Why do the people inside look so natural? | They don't know anybody is watching | They are actors putting on a play for the whole village | They are asleep | They are posing for a painting
exercise: How do Rat and Mole feel watching the cosy homes? | Wistful — far from home themselves | Jealous and angry at the people | Bored | Frightened of being seen
exercise: What is behind the one window with its blind drawn? | The shadow of a bird-cage with a sleepy bird | A family eating a huge Christmas dinner | A dog barking | A cat looking out
exercise: What tiny thing in the cage can they see? | Yesterday's lump of sugar | A little silver bell hanging from the top | A hard-boiled egg for its breakfast | A tiny round mirror to peck at
exercise: What wakes them «as from a dream»? | A sting of frozen sleet | A dog barking at them from a gate | Church bells | The bird singing
exercise: What were the Mole's thoughts mostly about on the dark road? | Supper | The Wild Wood and its faces | Toad's new car | Swimming
exercise: How does the Rat walk? | A little ahead, shoulders humped, eyes on the road | Arm in arm with the Mole, talking all the time | Behind | Running
exercise: What does the writer say is the only word people have for the animal's many feelings in the nose? | Smell | Taste | Sniff | Touch
exercise: How does the call of home first reach the Mole? | Like an electric shock, through his nose | As a sound, like a bell far away across the fields | As a letter | In a dream
exercise: What is the call, once the Mole remembers? | His old home, very close by | The Badger's house | Toad Hall | The river
exercise: How did the Mole's home seem to feel about him? | Sad and missing him, but not angry | Furious that he had left it dirty and half whitewashed | Happy without him | It didn't care
exercise: What does the Mole shout to the Rat? | «It's my home, my old home!» | «Help! The weasels are after me!» | «Wait, I'm hungry» | «Let's go to the village»
exercise: Why doesn't the Rat stop? | He's far ahead, worried about snow and the way | He is angry with the Mole and won't listen | He is deaf | He wants to get to the Badger's
exercise: What does the Rat promise? | To come for it tomorrow | To buy the Mole a new house | Nothing at all | To go back with him right now
exercise: What else is the Rat smelling? | Approaching snow | Supper cooking in the village | The river, not far off | Weasels in the hedges
exercise: Why does the Rat say he needs the Mole? | He wants his nose to find the way | He wants him to carry the heavy bag | He's lonely | He's lost his lantern
exercise: What does the Mole do in the end? | Follows the Rat, out of loyalty | Runs off alone to his home without a word | Sits down and refuses to move | Goes back to the village
test: Lily: «the Mole goes off home on his own cos Rat's being a pain.» Put it right. | He would never leave his friend, so he follows the Rat | Right: he leaves the Rat on the road and runs off home in the dark | He goes home with the Otter | He goes to the Badger's
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Rat heard the Mole clearly and said no just to be mean.» | False: he was too far ahead to hear the pain in his voice | True: he heard every word and laughed at him | True, he hates Mole's home | False: the Rat stopped and went back at once
test: Josh from Leeds, proper nesh: «it's freezing, I'd have gone in one o' them cottages.» (nesh = feels the cold) Why don't Rat and Mole go into a cottage? | Animals don't mix with villages; they only look in | They aren't allowed to, because the doors are locked | They have no money | They do — they eat with a family
test: «Spectators … moving at will from one theatre to another.» What are the «theatres»? | The lit-up cottage windows | The village halls with plays | The steaming sheep-pens by the road | Toad's new car parked in the lane
test: Why does the bird in the cage make them feel the «sense of home» most of all? | It is a tiny, safe, warm world shut away from the cold outside | Because they want to eat it | Because it sings them a song | Because it is the Badger's bird
test: Looking back: in chapter I, how did the Mole leave his home? | He threw down his brush, said «Hang spring-cleaning!» and bolted out | He packed his things carefully into a bag, locked the door, and left the key with a rabbit | The Rat came to fetch him | A flood washed him out
test: Looking back: in chapter II, a bird in a bird-cage was in trouble. What happened to it? | It was in the smashed caravan, sobbing and calling to be let out | It flew away over the downs, singing, and was never seen again by anyone | It sang all the way home | It was eaten
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did the Rat say at night in the caravan about his river? | «I don't talk about my river … But I think about it—all the time!» | «I never want to see that dull old river again — the open road is the life for me» | «Let's go home tomorrow» | «My river is the best in the world»
test: Looking back: in chapter II, the Mole whispered an offer to the homesick Rat. What was it? | To run away very early and go back to the river | To buy the Rat a new blue and white boat when they got home again | To leave Toad on the road | To sing him a song
test: So in chapter II the Mole was kind to the homesick Rat. What happens now in chapter V? | The Mole is homesick, and the Rat at first doesn't notice | The Rat is homesick again, and the Mole ignores him and keeps on walking | Nobody is homesick | Both go to Toad Hall
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did the Mole say about sticking with the Rat? | «I'll always stick to you, Rat» | «I'll do as I like» | «Toad is my best friend now» | «I'm going home»
test: What would have happened if the Rat had heard the Mole's cry clearly? | We think he'd have stopped — he is kind, as we see often | He would have run away from the Mole even faster, to get home before the snow came | He would have laughed | He would have called the Badger
test: Ivy, lowkey teary: «I'm dead, why's she crying over a smell?» (I'm dead = that's so funny) What does the smell really mean to the Mole? | His own home calling him, full of memories | A tasty supper cooking somewhere nearby, in one of the cottages | A danger hiding somewhere in the dark fields | A flower blooming under the snow by the hedge
test: Nate from Toronto, in his toque (woolly hat): «why does the writer go on about «smell», it's just a sniff, eh?» What is the writer saying? | Animals feel far more through the nose than we have words for | People smell far better than animals do, and have lots of words for it | Smells are not important | Moles cannot smell
test: ★★★ «He had lost what he could hardly be said to have found.» How can you lose something you have hardly found? | He found it only by smell, for a moment, then left it | He lost the key to his house somewhere in the snow | He never had a home | He found a coin and dropped it
test: ★★★ The Mole's home calls him «with no bitterness or anger». Which word in this list would the home NOT feel? | Bitter | Sad | Missing him | Wanting him back
test: ★★★ Track — path — lane — road. Put these in order from smallest to biggest, as in the chapter. | Track, path, lane, road | Road, lane, path, track | Path, track, road, lane | Lane, track, road, path
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the Latin title «Dulce Domum». | Sweet home | Sweet sleep under the cold winter stars | The open road | The river bank
exam: Identify the season of the year in which the events of the chapter take place. | Mid-December | The early part of summer, at the opening of the boating season | Spring | Autumn
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Rat's failure to halt in response to the Mole's appeal? | Distance, and preoccupation with the weather and the way | A deliberate and unkind refusal to attend to the Mole's evident distress | Deafness | Fear of the village
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Mole's conduct upon the Rat's refusal. | He followed the Rat, his loyalty standing firm | He abandoned the Rat and proceeded to his former dwelling alone | He returned to the village | He remained upon the road
